3. Why a curated table instead of a live macro-calendar API — read this before objecting

A genuinely comprehensive, always-current economic calendar (Trading Economics, Forex Factory, Investing.com) either requires a paid API key, has an unstable/scraping-only free tier, or both. Building this phase's blackoutActive signal on top of a flaky or paywalled third party would make the one part of this phase meant to be unquestionably real (the macro blackout window) the least reliable part of the system — worse than the honest stub it's replacing. Instead:

FOMC meeting dates are published by the Federal Reserve a year in advance and change extremely rarely. NFP (Non-Farm Payrolls) is always the first Friday of the month at 8:30am ET. CPI release dates are published by the BLS months in advance.
Seed macro_events with the actual, real, currently-known 2025–2026 FOMC meeting dates and the real monthly NFP/CPI release pattern for the next 12 months from whenever you run the seed script. Look these up for real (Federal Reserve's own published FOMC calendar, BLS's own published release schedule) — do not fabricate plausible-looking dates. If you cannot verify a specific date with a real source at build time, mark it in a code comment as needing verification rather than inventing one.
This is a legitimate, bounded, honest engineering choice for this phase's scope — not a shortcut. State this reasoning in your final report, exactly as Phase 16 was required to state its own honesty caveat about synthetic upstream data. This phase removes one instance of that caveat (real news sentiment) and is explicit about the scope of the other (curated, not live, macro calendar) rather than overclaiming.
