6. Task 4 — The shared blackout-detection helper

New function, co-located in apps/engine/app/data/news_ingest.py or a new small apps/engine/app/data/macro_blackout.py (your choice, but only one implementation — all three new nodes above call this one function, never reimplement the window-check logic three times, the same "shared plumbing" discipline Phase 16 established for the LLM gateway):

python
async def compute_blackout(reference_time: datetime, db: Optional[AsyncSession]) -> tuple[bool, Optional[str]]:
    """
    Returns (blackout_active, reason). Queries macro_events for any row where
    reference_time falls within [scheduled_at - blackout_before_minutes,
    scheduled_at + blackout_after_minutes]. If db is None (pure in-memory historical
    pass with no DB session available, per Phase 14's design), fall back to an
    in-process cache of macro_events loaded once per backtest run rather than skipping
    the check entirely — a historical backtest should still respect real macro blackout
    windows for backtest realism, it just can't hit the DB per-bar.
    """

Handle the db is None case correctly — this matters. Per your Step Zero audit finding #6, historical/walk-forward/monte-carlo backtest passes run with ctx.db = None to keep the hot loop DB-free. Do not simply return (False, None) when db is None — that would silently make blackout detection a paper/live-only feature and contradict the entire point of testing a strategy's behavior around real macro events historically. Instead:

At the start of backtest_runner.py's run_backtest() (or simulate_historical_pass, wherever ordered_nodes/node_instances are built once per run — check your audit of this file), do a single upfront query loading all macro_events rows into an in-memory list, and pass that list down into the NodeContext as a new optional field (e.g. ctx.macro_events_cache: Optional[list]), exactly the same pattern already used for ctx.historical_window (a pre-fetched DataFrame passed once, not re-queried per bar).
compute_blackout checks ctx.macro_events_cache first if present (historical modes), else falls back to a real DB query if ctx.db is available (paper/live modes) — either path produces a correct, real answer; the difference is only about when the data is fetched (once upfront vs. per-tick), matching the existing candles vs. historical_window design you're extending, not inventing a new architectural pattern.
Update NodeContext in apps/engine/app/nodes/base.py to add this new optional field with a safe None default, and thread it through bar_runner.py's run_one_bar and build_node_instances the same way Phase 14 threaded through mode/user_id/db/etc. — additive, behavior-preserving for every node that doesn't use it.
